## 7. Chapter Two: Looking Beyond the Resume

The second dashboard page, **Candidate & Skills Analysis**, moves from overall recruitment outcomes to candidate characteristics.

A resume contains more than an education label. Candidates may differ in academic performance, internships, project experience, certifications, research activity, and skills-related scores.

This page explores those characteristics individually and compares selected and not-selected candidates where appropriate.

### Education and academic performance

CGPA distribution helps describe the academic profiles represented in the dataset. University-tier comparisons provide another way to examine candidate composition and recorded outcomes.

These comparisons should not be interpreted as proof that a particular institution or CGPA causes a hiring decision.

### Experience and practical exposure

Internship and project counts provide indicators of recorded practical exposure. Experience groups help summarize how candidates are distributed across different levels of professional experience.

Comparing selection rates across these groups can reveal differences worth investigating further.

### Skills and professional achievements

Technical skills scores and soft-skills scores are compared by hiring status. Certification counts, programming-language counts, hackathons, and research papers provide additional dimensions for understanding the candidate population.

These are indicators available in the dataset, not a complete representation of a person's abilities or potential.

